theory.intro:
A **speed-time graph** plots speed against time, so its gradient is the **acceleration** — the rate at which the speed changes.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3.6);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(6.6,0);
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0.3)--(1.8,2.4)--(4.0,2.4)--(5.8,0.3);
\node[font=\small] at (1.35,3.05) {Speeding up};
\node[font=\small] at (2.9,1.2) {Constant speed};
\node[font=\small] at (6.2,1.9) {Slowing down};
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.5,1.8) {Speed};
\node[font=\small] at (3.3,-0.55) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
The graph shows a car's journey. Calculate its acceleration during each of the five stages, and state when it is stationary.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=2.5mm,y=2mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{graphblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[gridgrey,very thin,xstep=5mm,ystep=8mm] (0,0) grid (24,16);
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(25,0);
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(0,18);
\foreach \t in {0,4,10,14,18,24} {\draw[black] (\t,0)--(\t,-0.4);\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\t,-0.4) {$\t$};}
\foreach \v in {4,8,12,16} {\draw[black] (0,\v)--(-0.4,\v);\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-0.4,\v) {$\v$};}
